course: dictionary
lesson: 15535
title: Suitably: the right way for the moment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Suitably is an adverb. It tells you how something is done, and it means in a way that is right or proper for the situation. Look at the word inside it: suitable means fitting or right, so suitably means in a suitable way. «Please come suitably dressed for the rain.» The clue is the rain: you wear a coat and boots, not sandals.
explain: The second meaning is about how much you would expect. «She was suitably impressed» means she was impressed by the amount you would expect for that thing. If the show was very good, she was impressed a lot; if it was just okay, she was impressed a little. The rest of the sentence tells you the size.
explain: Try the check: swap in a simpler phrase and see if the sentence still makes sense. «We were suitably dressed» can become «we were properly dressed». If the meaning stays the same, you have got it.
fact: Suitably means in a way that is right or proper for the situation.
fact: Suitable is the adjective, and suitably is the adverb that goes with it.
fact: «Suitably dressed» means wearing the right clothes for the weather or the event.
fact: «Suitably impressed» means impressed as much as you would expect.
fact: School letters often say «Pupils should come suitably dressed for outdoor learning» or «wear suitable shoes».
exercise: «The children were suitably dressed for the snowy playground.» What does suitably mean here? | In old clothes that are torn | In a loud and bright way | *In the right clothes for the weather | Quickly, before the snow came
exercise: «The judge was suitably impressed by the science project.» What does suitably add to impressed? | Angrily | Not at all | Very slowly | *As much as you would expect
exercise: Ms Patel writes, «Pupils should come suitably dressed for outdoor learning.» What is she asking them to do? | Bring a pet to school | Leave their coats at home | Wear their best party clothes | *Wear clothes that are right for being outside
test: In «The nurse was suitably qualified for the job», what does suitably mean? | *In the right way, with the training the job needs | Quickly, in a rush | Not qualified at all | Loudly and proudly
test: Which sentence uses suitably to mean as much as you would expect? | *«The class was suitably pleased with their marks.» | «The mountain was suitably tall for a hike.» | «The river ran suitably fast under the bridge.» | «He suitably dropped his bag and ran.»
exam: Which word is the adverb form of suitable? | suitcase | *suitably | suitability | suitor
